From: well0nez <well0nez@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] soc: sunxi: cpu_comm: register callbacks off the bring-up path

Reinstates the routine registration that 0098 took back, in the one place
and with the one lock scope that the three failed attempts on 07.09. rule
out -- and only there. What it does NOT do is claim to have found the
reason those attempts failed; that reason is not established, and
vorschlaege/callback2/BEFUND.md says so at length. This is a registration
that cannot cost a probe, plus the instrument that can decide the open
question at the device.

The gap itself is unchanged and real: cpu_comm_register_callback() only
ever filled a driver-internal table, nobody put a descriptor into the
routine table in shared memory, and the firmware's SendComm2CPUEx() gives
up with -3 in FindRoutine() before it touches the message FIFO
(cpu_comm_proto.c:230). No descriptor, no call on the wire, handler count
stays 0 while the MIPS elog shows the callback firing.

Three things are different from the version 0098 reverted.

1. The registration leaves the probe.

   sun50i-h713-hdmirx now arms both callbacks on the first open() of the
   video node and disarms them on the last close(). The events they feed
   are V4L2 source-change events, which reach subscribed file handles and
   nothing else, so before the first open() there is nobody they could
   reach -- and a failed open() costs a retry, where a failed probe costs
   a cold start. The probe is byte-for-byte the probe that A-H were
   accepted with: twenty-two calls out, no descriptor in.

   This also makes the open question cheap to ask. Arming and disarming
   the co-processor's sender is now an ordinary file operation, so
   "does installing a descriptor disturb the RPC path" can be tested
   twenty times in a row instead of once per cold start -- and
   /sys/kernel/debug/cpu_comm/watch can do it with no driver at all.

2. Shared memory is touched with cpu_comm_kcb_mutex dropped.

   The receive path takes that mutex, and command_action() sends the MIPS
   its acknowledgement only after the receive path returns
   (cpu_comm_proto.c, cpu_comm_kernel_deliver() then SendAckLow()). The
   reverted version held the mutex across AddInRoutine(), which takes SW
   spinlock 2 -- the lock the MIPS takes for the same table in its own
   AddInRoutine (display.bin @0x8B11C4C4) and waits for without a timeout
   (re/notes/display.bin-init-chain.md). Holding a mutex that gates the
   acknowledgement across a lock the other processor is waiting on is a
   shape worth removing whether or not it caused anything. Registration
   now reserves a slot under the mutex, drops it, installs the
   descriptor, and takes the mutex again only to publish the handler;
   delivery treats the fn pointer as the valid marker, so a reserved slot
   is invisible to it. Unregistration and the debugfs listing are split
   the same way.

   Delivery keeps the counters that made the gap visible, but only takes
   the mutex when something is actually registered, so the idle receive
   path costs one copy and two atomics as it did before 0092.

3. A lock that was not acquired is no longer indistinguishable from one
   that was.

   comm_SpinLock() throws spinLock()'s return value away
   (cpu_comm_hw.c), so AddInRoutine() modifies the shared table even when
   the lock timed out after 10001 retries -- which has been measured once
   ("spinLock(2, mode=2): TIMEOUT ... owner=2 thread_id=0x8b8f3160
   refcnt=1"). Repairing that means touching the base port, so instead
   this refuses to walk into a contended lock (-EBUSY, retry) and reads
   the entry back through FindRoutineEx() afterwards, so "AddInRoutine
   returned 0" and "the descriptor is in the table" stop being the same
   statement.

The debugfs file grows two lines that exist to be able to fail: the
routine table's version/count header, and the state of SW spinlock 2. A
non-zero refcnt with a thread id and nothing running is exactly the
wedged state in which the MIPS waits forever, and it is otherwise only
reachable through /dev/mem.

The API takes the routine's name instead of its id, as the reverted
version did and for the same reason: the descriptor carries the name, and
there is no way back from the hash.

Signed-off-by: well0nez <well0nez@users.noreply.github.com>
---
diff -ruN a/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/sun50i-h713-hdmirx.c b/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/sun50i-h713-hdmirx.c
--- a/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/sun50i-h713-hdmirx.c	2026-09-07 11:15:00.000000000 +0200
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/sun50i-h713-hdmirx.c	2026-09-07 11:45:00.000000000 +0200
@@ -40,6 +40,11 @@
  *  - The firmware announces signal loss and signal return on its own through
  *    the MipsHalCallback_SignalChange callback (same measurement). The V4L2
  *    source change event hangs off that callback; nothing polls for it.
+ *    That callback is armed by the first open() of the video node and
+ *    disarmed by the last close(), not by the probe -- arming it means
+ *    writing a descriptor into shared memory under a lock the MIPS waits
+ *    on, and that is not a thing to do in the middle of a bring-up
+ *    sequence. See h713_hdmirx_register_callbacks().
  *
  * Documentation: doku/88-v4l2-hdmirx.md, doku/78-nachtplan-hdmi-switch.md
  * appendix A (the seven stages of a source change).
@@ -391,6 +396,8 @@
 	struct video_device vdev;
 	struct vb2_queue queue;
 	struct mutex lock;		/* serialises ioctl and queue */
+	struct mutex cb_lock;		/* serialises cb_users below */
+	unsigned int cb_users;		/* open file handles, see _open() */
 	struct dentry *debugfs;
 
 	void __iomem *shm;		/* cpu_comm shared memory window */
@@ -590,12 +597,13 @@
 	};
 
 	/*
-	 * The firmware is already calling back while probe is still running:
-	 * the bring-up sequence arms SignalChange and HotPlugByPort long
-	 * before the video device exists, and the EDID/HPD pulse right after
-	 * it provokes exactly those two events. video_register_device() is
-	 * what initialises vdev->fh_list and vdev->fh_lock, so queueing an
-	 * event before it walks a list whose head is still NULL.
+	 * video_register_device() is what initialises vdev->fh_list and
+	 * vdev->fh_lock, so queueing an event before it walks a list whose
+	 * head is still NULL. The callbacks are registered from open() and
+	 * therefore cannot fire before that call, which leaves this guard
+	 * for the other end: h713_hdmirx_remove() takes the video node down
+	 * before it drops the callbacks, so a call the firmware had already
+	 * put on the wire can still arrive here afterwards.
 	 */
 	if (!video_is_registered(&rx->vdev))
 		return;
@@ -1500,10 +1508,133 @@
 	.vidioc_unsubscribe_event	= v4l2_event_unsubscribe,
 };
 
+/*
+ * The two routines the firmware calls back into. Registering them is not
+ * only a matter of this driver: cpu_comm_register_callback() installs a
+ * descriptor for the name in the routine table in shared memory, and
+ * without that descriptor the firmware's own name lookup fails and it
+ * never sends the call.
+ *
+ * Which is why this does not happen in probe(). Two properties of the act
+ * decide that, and both are in the cpu_comm header:
+ *
+ *  - the descriptor is what arms the sender, so from the moment it is in
+ *    the table the firmware can call in, on the cpu_comm receive work
+ *    item, in parallel with whatever this driver is doing;
+ *  - installing it writes shared memory under SW spinlock 2, the lock the
+ *    MIPS takes for the same table and waits for without a timeout.
+ *
+ * A probe is the worst place for both: it runs twenty-two synchronous
+ * RPCs and then the ARISC EDID and hotplug sequence, all of which need
+ * the co-processor to answer, and when it fails the only way back is a
+ * cold start. On 07.09. three attempts to register inside the probe were
+ * put on the board and none of them completed it -- the third one failed
+ * one step *later* although its registration had been moved behind
+ * video_register_device() and was never reached, which is what took the
+ * registration off the list of explanations and put the *place* of it on
+ * that list instead (mainline/patches/vorschlaege/callback2/BEFUND.md).
+ *
+ * open() is where a caller is ready to be called and where a failure is
+ * survivable: the events these handlers produce are V4L2 source-change
+ * events, which go to subscribed file handles, so before the first open()
+ * there is nobody they could reach, and an -EBUSY from a contended
+ * routine table is a failed open() that userspace can simply repeat.
+ */
+#define H713_HDMIRX_CB_SIGNAL	"MipsHalCallback_SignalChange"
+#define H713_HDMIRX_CB_HOTPLUG	"MipsHalCallback_HdmiHotPlugByPortHandler"
+
+static int h713_hdmirx_register_callbacks(struct h713_hdmirx *rx)
+{
+	int ret;
+
+	ret = cpu_comm_register_callback(H713_HDMIRX_CB_SIGNAL,
+					 h713_hdmirx_signal_cb, rx);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
+	ret = cpu_comm_register_callback(H713_HDMIRX_CB_HOTPLUG,
+					 h713_hdmirx_hotplug_cb, rx);
+	if (ret) {
+		cpu_comm_unregister_callback(H713_HDMIRX_CB_SIGNAL,
+					     h713_hdmirx_signal_cb);
+		return ret;
+	}
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+static void h713_hdmirx_unregister_callbacks(struct h713_hdmirx *rx)
+{
+	cpu_comm_unregister_callback(H713_HDMIRX_CB_SIGNAL,
+				     h713_hdmirx_signal_cb);
+	cpu_comm_unregister_callback(H713_HDMIRX_CB_HOTPLUG,
+				     h713_hdmirx_hotplug_cb);
+}
+
+/*
+ * First open registers, last close unregisters. The count is the number
+ * of open file handles, not of subscriptions: a handle that has not
+ * subscribed costs nothing but the descriptor, and tying the descriptor
+ * to VIDIOC_SUBSCRIBE_EVENT would put the shared-memory write inside an
+ * ioctl that userspace expects to be free.
+ */
+static int h713_hdmirx_open(struct file *file)
+{
+	struct h713_hdmirx *rx = video_drvdata(file);
+	int ret;
+
+	ret = v4l2_fh_open(file);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
+	mutex_lock(&rx->cb_lock);
+	if (!rx->cb_users) {
+		ret = h713_hdmirx_register_callbacks(rx);
+		if (ret) {
+			mutex_unlock(&rx->cb_lock);
+			dev_warn(rx->dev,
+				 "Callbacks nicht registrierbar (%d) -- open abgelehnt, Wiederholung ist gefahrlos\n",
+				 ret);
+			v4l2_fh_release(file);
+			return ret;
+		}
+	}
+	rx->cb_users++;
+	mutex_unlock(&rx->cb_lock);
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+static int h713_hdmirx_release(struct file *file)
+{
+	struct h713_hdmirx *rx = video_drvdata(file);
+	int ret;
+
+	ret = vb2_fop_release(file);
+
+	mutex_lock(&rx->cb_lock);
+	if (rx->cb_users && !--rx->cb_users)
+		h713_hdmirx_unregister_callbacks(rx);
+	mutex_unlock(&rx->cb_lock);
+
+	return ret;
+}
+
+/* Whatever is still open when the driver goes away. */
+static void h713_hdmirx_drop_callbacks(struct h713_hdmirx *rx)
+{
+	mutex_lock(&rx->cb_lock);
+	if (rx->cb_users) {
+		rx->cb_users = 0;
+		h713_hdmirx_unregister_callbacks(rx);
+	}
+	mutex_unlock(&rx->cb_lock);
+}
+
 static const struct v4l2_file_operations h713_hdmirx_fops = {
 	.owner		= THIS_MODULE,
-	.open		= v4l2_fh_open,
-	.release	= vb2_fop_release,
+	.open		= h713_hdmirx_open,
+	.release	= h713_hdmirx_release,
 	.poll		= vb2_fop_poll,
 	.unlocked_ioctl	= video_ioctl2,
 	.mmap		= vb2_fop_mmap,
@@ -1677,40 +1808,6 @@
 	return 0;
 }
 
